AUDIO: 'want to take and then you\'ll be creating your own unique character from that face. So here we can see that the face has generated. I ran the workflow and this is the output that I got and it looks good. You can see that most of the facial features look pretty different'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'than the original reference photo. Obviously, we still have the same eye color and so on, but if I wanted to stray away further from the original reference photo\'s face, I could just go up in the D noiseis here. And if I wanted to be a little bit closer to the original reference photos face, then I'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can just go down in the D noiseis as well. So depending on how accurate to the original reference photo you want the face to be, you can just mess around with that D noiseis value, but I\'m going to use this because I think this is fine. So on screen now is the data set creation workflow that I\'m going to be'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using to create the entire data set that I will use to train the Laura. This workflow runs on two models. I\'ve included the download links to the models as markdown notes that are actually inside of the workflow so that way you can download all the models that'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ll need. But the first model that we\'re going to be using is Flux 2 Klein 9B. And this handles all the actual image editing, which is taking your reference face and placing it into each pose and outfit for the prompts and putting it on the reference body. But'

[04:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Flux 2 Klein on its own struggles with fine skin detail. So there\'s actually a second pass which is further on down the line in the pipeline of the workflow. And this second pass uses Z image turbo at a very low D noiseise to sharpen the'

[05:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'skin detail because it does really well at actually creating good realism. And Z image turbo is also feeding into the face and eye detailer subgraph here. And we can see in this subgraph that it\'s'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using the YOLO V face detector and eye detector from Eiffel V2. And this pass through is basically just to recover the skin texture that Flux 2 client loses. You\'re going to drop your reference'

[05:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'photos into this workflow. We can see here that one load image is for our face selfie and the second load image is for the body. And I recommend cropping out the face in the second load image pass that loads the body. So that way there\'s no confusion for the model when creating'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the fully polished full body POV images. And for the actual pose list after you drop your reference photos in, I\'ve got a 50 prompt master list built for data set generation. So, this has 15 face'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'selfies, 25 frontward- facing shots, and 10 backward facing shots. And that mix isn\'t random. It\'s based on how character lor is actually learned. So, if you have too many close-ups and the Laura nails the face, but the body falls apart in full body generations, that\'s'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because you didn\'t have enough full body POV images in your data set. And if there\'s too few backward facing shots and it has no idea what the back of your character or their outfit looks like, then it\'s also going to struggle with that. But this split gives it enough of each to generalize instead of'

[06:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'overfitting to one angle. But before you commit to the full 50, there\'s a fiveprompt quick test in the prompt list node by default. So you can run some test generations on only five photos instead of 50, so you can make sure the character looks how you want it to.'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There\'s one prompt per major category. So once you\'ve uploaded the face and body reference photos, run this first. Check that the face and body hold up the way you want to and then paste the full 50 in when you\'re content. And one more thing on this workflow, there\'s a prepen'

[06:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'field on the CR prompt list that I\'ve just pulled up. And by default, it just has the reference photo calls, but if you want to push the body proportions stronger than what comes out naturally, that\'s something you can add into the prepen field yourself. And I have a template that exaggerates body features'

[07:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here in a markdown note that you will just add on to this prepen text field. So, here it is right here. And I have that. If you want to just go for an insane body, basically what you can do is copy and paste this last bit of this'

[07:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'markdown note. Copy this, come over to the prompt list node, and basically just paste it onto the end of this prepen text. And what that will do is apply those body modifications to each and'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
